--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components, and the license boundary
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The pure-JavaScript engine core (src/core, src/mcp, src/harness) is
Apache-2.0. The following native analysis lanes are OPTIONAL and, when
present, live in SEPARATE subprojects under their own licenses. They are
NOT linked into the Apache-2.0 core and MUST be distributed in a relinkable
form: no fat or shadow jar, no single Docker image, so that a recipient can
replace one of them without rebuilding the core.

  - Java source lane (Eclipse JDT Core)         EPL-2.0
  - JVM dataflow lane (SootUp)                   LGPL-2.1   [separate subproject]
  - SQL lane (SQLGlot)                           MIT

Comparator/PoC components (e.g. WALA) are excluded from the public scope.

The web lane does NOT use the TypeScript Compiler API, and an earlier version
of this file said it did. It parses with @babel/parser, which is pure
JavaScript under the MIT license and is VENDORED into this repository at
adapters/web/vendor/babel-parser.cjs (credited below).

The Java and the SQL lanes resolve nothing from a package registry at build
time either: the Java lane compiles one source file with the JDK the user
already has, and the SQL lane runs in a Python virtual environment the user
creates. So this repository fetches nothing to build and nothing to analyze,
and the two things it does carry from somebody else are the viewer's browser
bundles and the web lane's parser, both listed below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Vendored third-party components (viewer/vendor)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The local web viewer (viewer/index.html, served by `cascade view`) ships two
pre-built MIT browser bundles. They are vendored, not fetched: the page is
served by a local process with no network access. They run only in the
browser — nothing in the engine, the MCP servers or the CLI imports them.

  - 3d-force-graph  1.80.0   MIT   Copyright (c) 2017 Vasco Asturiano
        viewer/vendor/3d-force-graph.min.js
        https://github.com/vasturiano/3d-force-graph
        licence: viewer/vendor/LICENSE-3d-force-graph.txt

  - force-graph      1.51.4  MIT   Copyright (c) 2018 Vasco Asturiano
        viewer/vendor/force-graph.min.js
        https://github.com/vasturiano/force-graph
        licence: viewer/vendor/LICENSE-force-graph.txt

  - three (three.js)  r183   MIT   Copyright (c) 2010-2025 three.js authors
        BUNDLED INSIDE viewer/vendor/3d-force-graph.min.js — there is no
        separate file. https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js — the MIT text is
        the same as the two above, with the three.js authors as copyright
        holders.

Both bundles also carry their own smaller MIT dependencies inline (d3-*,
kapsule, accessor-fn, bezier-js, @tweenjs/tween.js, canvas-color-tracker,
index-array-by, lodash-es, three-forcegraph, three-render-objects). Exact
versions, sizes and upstream URLs: viewer/vendor/README.md.

The viewer also ships three Latin web-font subsets, for the same reason as the
bundles: the page is read on a machine that may have no network, and a typeface
must not depend on somebody else's uptime. They are used only by the browser.

  - IBM Plex Sans   OFL-1.1   Copyright 2017 IBM Corp.
        viewer/vendor/fonts/IBM-Plex-Sans-latin.woff2
        (a VARIABLE font: one file covers weights 400-600)
        https://github.com/IBM/plex
        licence: viewer/vendor/fonts/OFL-IBM-Plex.txt

  - IBM Plex Mono   OFL-1.1   Copyright 2017 IBM Corp.
        viewer/vendor/fonts/IBM-Plex-Mono-400-latin.woff2
        viewer/vendor/fonts/IBM-Plex-Mono-500-latin.woff2
        https://github.com/IBM/plex
        licence: viewer/vendor/fonts/OFL-IBM-Plex.txt

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Vendored third-party components (adapters/web/vendor)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The web lane's worker (adapters/web/webfacts.mjs) parses JavaScript,
TypeScript, JSX, TSX and Vue single-file component script blocks. It does that
with one vendored, pure-JavaScript parser. It is vendored, not fetched: this
engine has no npm install step, and analysis makes no network call at all.
The bundle runs inside the worker process and nothing else in the engine
imports it.

  - @babel/parser  7.29.8  MIT  Copyright (C) 2012-2014 by various contributors
        adapters/web/vendor/babel-parser.cjs
        https://github.com/babel/babel (packages/babel-parser)
        the published tarball's package/lib/index.js, copied byte for byte; the
        .cjs extension is the only change, because this repository's
        package.json is "type": "module"
        licence: adapters/web/vendor/LICENSE-babel-parser.txt

The tarball URL it was copied from, the exact size, the sha256 and the reason
for the version choice are in adapters/web/vendor/README.md, and
test/gates.test.mjs holds the file to that hash.
